{"case_id":"C029","gap_exists":true,"gap_type":["entry_point","API protocol","state","configuration","input semantics","attack surface"],"commit_induced":"YES","maintenance_needed":true,"confidence":99,"affected_functions":["hasher.update!","hasher.update_bitvec256!","hasher.checksum_bitvec256","hasher.up!","hasher.get_quirk","hasher.set_quirk!"],"reason":"S1 adds a new SHA-256 hasher API and implementation, but H0 is exclusively a JSON decoder harness and does not compile or invoke the SHA-256 module. Consequently, fuzz bytes cannot exercise SHA-256 input processing, stateful updates, buffered block boundaries, or checksum finalization. S0 had no matching SHA-256 declaration, so this uncovered attack surface was introduced by the commit rather than inherited historically.","evidence":["H0 defines WUFFS_CONFIG__MODULE__JSON and WUFFS_CONFIG__MODULE__AUX__JSON, but no SHA-256 module configuration.","H0 reaches only wuffs_json__decoder__decode_tokens and wuffs_aux::DecodeJson through fuzz_complex, fuzz_simple, and fuzz_cpp.","S1 creates std/sha256/common_sha256.wuffs with public struct hasher and public functions hasher.update!, hasher.update_bitvec256!, and hasher.checksum_bitvec256.","hasher.update! maintains length_modulo_u64, length_overflows_u64, buf_len, buf_data, and h0 through h7 across calls, requiring stateful chunk-sequence coverage absent from H0.","hasher.checksum_bitvec256 handles padding and one- versus two-block finalization, behavior that no H0 path invokes.","The selected S0 context states that there was no matching declaration, while all of this SHA-256 surface is new in S1."],"recommended_action":"Add a SHA-256 fuzz harness that enables the SHA-256 module, maps fuzz input to varied sequences of hasher.update! calls including 55/56/63/64-byte boundaries and empty chunks, invokes both checksum_bitvec256 and update_bitvec256!, and checks equivalent digests across different chunkings."}
